+#if defined(XNU_NATIVE_VIRTIO_CONSOLE) && XNU_NATIVE_VIRTIO_CONSOLE
+		/* EVERY PROCESS EXIT UP TO A BOUND, because the exec record proved
+		 * what started and nothing observes what finished.
+		 *
+		 * MEASURED 2026-11-03 on build 41646368244's guest:
+		 *
+		 *   {"event":"exec.seen","n":1,"comm":"launchd",...}
+		 *   {"event":"exec.seen","n":2,"comm":"apfs_boot_util",...}
+		 *
+		 * Two execs against a bound of twelve, so launchd DID spawn -- it is
+		 * not stuck before its first job -- and then nothing.  apfs_boot_util
+		 * is n=2, which means it was exec'd after launchd started; a process
+		 * the kernel spawned during bsd_init() would be n=1.  No reference to
+		 * it exists in this tree, so launchd ran it from a plist.
+		 *
+		 * WHAT THAT CANNOT SAY, and the reason this exists: whether it
+		 * RETURNED.  The exec record proves a process started.  Nothing on the
+		 * channel observes an exit, so a boot that stops after
+		 * apfs_boot_util is equally consistent with it hanging inside that
+		 * binary and with it returning and launchd stalling afterwards.
+		 * Those are different findings with different next steps, and the fork
+		 * has to be measured rather than argued -- the last three forks were,
+		 * and each one was wrong the first time.
+		 *
+		 * BOUNDED at kMaxExitRecords, and the first bound of twelve was
+		 * exactly what made the previous conclusion wrong: it saturated at
+		 * dirs_cleaner and hid everything after it, which is where the
+		 * interesting processes are.  Sixty-four now, for the same reason the
+		 * exec record's bound went to sixty-four -- the channel's send()
+		 * waits synchronously on a 2000 ms deadline
+		 * (NativeVirtioConsole.cpp:226 sets it, :250 is the wait), but the
+		 * measured pace is one record per eight seconds of guest time, which
+		 * is the boot's own and not the transport's.
+		 *
+		 * AND SIXTY-FOUR SATURATED IN INTERVAL 42.  exit.seen
+		 * stopped at exactly n=64 and the 64th record is
+		 * `exit.seen n=64 comm='bluetoothd' code=256`, while
+		 * bluetoothd kept exiting afterwards -- it spawned 35 times in
+		 * the whole boot and is the last record in the capture.  Every
+		 * exit after the 64th is therefore invisible, which is why
+		 * exit.seen reports 64 while spawn.seen reports 155 in the same
+		 * file.  This is the same defect that hid 91 of interval 41's 155
+		 * spawns on the spawn emitter: that bound went 64 -> 512
+		 * afterwards and this one was left behind.  Five hundred and twelve
+		 * now, which at interval 42's pace of one exit per eight seconds
